<html>
<title>Save</title>
<body>


<h1>Save</h1>

<p>
To save all or part of memory from the <strong>Memory Dump</strong> tab, use the <strong>Save</strong> button (1) shown below. The current selected <a href="DumpMemoryType.htm">memory type</a> is saved.
</p>

<p>
<img src="images/dump_save.png" border="0" width="724" height="609">
</p>
<ul>
<li>(1) <strong>Save</strong> button</li>
<li>(2) <strong>Start address</strong> text field</li>
<li>(3) <strong>End address</strong> text field</li>
</ul>

<p>
For all types, the complete memory space is saved, i.e., 64K in CPU mode (assuming no start or end address is specified); for video and other modes the size depends on the configured memory size.
</p>

<p>
For CPU memory, to save part of the CPU memory specify the <strong>Start address</strong> (2) and <strong>End address</strong> (3).
</p>

<p>
To save in binary format use file extension <tt>bin</tt>, <tt>rom</tt>, <tt>ram</tt>, <tt>cos</tt>, <tt>c8</tt>, <tt>ch8</tt>, <tt>c8x</tt>, <tt>ch10</tt> or <tt>sc8</tt>. To save in Intel hex format use file extension <tt>hex</tt>. Any other extension saves in text format (i.e., RCA Elf Emulator hex format).
</p>
</body>
</html>
